[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Bundle Size Optimization & Code Splitting in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As modern web applications grow, managing JavaScript bundle size becomes critical for performance. Svelte compiles down to small, framework-less vanilla JavaScript, naturally producing smaller bundles than React or Angular. However, as your app scales, implementing code splitting ensures users only download the code they need for the current view."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Svelte and Code Splitting"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, bundlers like Vite (which SvelteKit uses) handle code splitting at the route level. In a standard Svelte Single Page Application (SPA), you can achieve manual component-level code splitting using dynamic imports combined with Svelte's asynchronous component handling or built-in await blocks."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Dynamic Imports"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To load a component lazily, use the dynamic import() syntax, which returns a Promise resolving to the component module. Here is how you can conditionally load a heavy component on user interaction:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  let HeavyComponent = null;
  let isLoading = false;

  async function loadHeavyComponent() {
    if (HeavyComponent) return;
    isLoading = true;
    try {
      const module = await import('./HeavyComponent.svelte');
      HeavyComponent = module.default;
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to load component', error);
    } finally {
      isLoading = false;
    }
  }
</script>

<button on:click={loadHeavyComponent}>
  Load Heavy Feature
</button>

{#if isLoading}
  <p>Loading...</p>
{/if}

{#if HeavyComponent}
  <svelte:component this={HeavyComponent} />
{/if}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging SvelteKit for Route-Level Splitting"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you are using SvelteKit, route-level code splitting is completely automatic. Every file placed in the src/routes directory is treated as a separate page and automatically bundled into its own chunk."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your root layout lightweight to avoid pulling heavy dependencies into every page."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use dynamic imports for modals, tabs, or components rendered below the fold."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Analyze your bundle using tools like rollup-plugin-visualizer to identify unexpected bloat."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Combining Svelte's lightweight compiler architecture with dynamic imports and SvelteKit's route-based splitting results in lightning-fast load times. Always profile your application bundle regularly to maintain optimal performance."}]
